const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"The PSAT/NMSQT is the practice SAT. It's digital, adaptive, and can qualify you for scholarships.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The PSAT/NMSQT mirrors the Digital SAT: a Reading and Writing section (two modules, 54 questions total) and a Math section (two modules, 44 questions total). Like the SAT, it's adaptive: your first module determines the difficulty of your second."},{"t":"p","text":"Scores range from 320 to 1520 (each section 160–760). The scale is slightly lower than the SAT's because it's designed for 10th and 11th graders."},{"t":"p","text":"A Desmos graphing calculator is built in for Math. There's no penalty for wrong answers."},{"t":"p","text":"Your score report shows your strengths and areas to improve, which is a great guide for SAT prep."}]},{"say":"In 11th grade, the PSAT/NMSQT can qualify you for National Merit recognition and scholarships.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Juniors' scores are converted into a Selection Index (calculated from your section scores). Students with the highest Selection Indexes in each state can become Commended Students or Semifinalists, and many go on to become Finalists and earn scholarships."},{"t":"p","text":"The qualifying Selection Index cutoff varies by state and year."},{"t":"p","text":"Even if you don't qualify for National Merit, the PSAT is valuable practice and can connect you with other scholarship programs."},{"t":"p","text":"Taking it in 10th grade is good practice; the 11th-grade score is the one that counts for National Merit."}]}],[{"say":"PSAT Reading and Writing uses short passages with one question each, just like the SAT.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Question types: words in context, text structure and purpose, cross-text connections, central ideas, evidence (from text and graphs), and inferences."},{"t":"p","text":"Strategy: read the question first, then the passage. Predict an answer before looking at choices. The correct answer is always directly supported by the text."},{"t":"p","text":"Eliminate traps: answers that are too extreme, out of scope, or only half right."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A passage says a new bridge design \"reduced costs but raised safety concerns.\" Which answer best describes the author's view?","steps":["The author notes both a benefit and a drawback"],"answer":"The design has advantages and disadvantages (not \"the design is a failure\")"}]},{"say":"Writing questions test grammar, punctuation and clear expression. These are some of the fastest points on the test.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Conventions: sentence boundaries (no comma splices), subject–verb agreement, pronoun agreement, verb tense and punctuation (semicolons, colons, apostrophes)."},{"t":"p","text":"Expression of Ideas: choose transitions that match the logic between sentences, and for rhetorical synthesis questions, pick the answer that uses the notes to meet the stated goal."},{"t":"ex","problem":"\"The experiment failed. ______, the team learned valuable lessons.\"","steps":["A contrast between failure and benefit"],"answer":"Nevertheless (or However)"},{"t":"tip","text":"Grammar questions are usually the quickest on the test. Answer them confidently to bank time for harder reading questions."}]}],[{"say":"PSAT Math covers the same four domains as the SAT, with a bit less difficulty.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Algebra: linear equations, inequalities, linear functions and systems. Advanced Math: quadratics, exponential functions, polynomials and function notation."},{"t":"p","text":"Key skills: interpret slope and intercepts in context, solve systems by substitution or elimination, factor quadratics, and use exponent rules."},{"t":"p","text":"Use the Desmos calculator to graph equations, find intersections and check answers."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In C = 15h + 40, C is the cost of a repair taking h hours. What does 40 represent?","steps":["When h = 0, C = 40"],"answer":"A fixed fee charged even for zero hours of work"},{"t":"tip","text":"Graph equations in Desmos to check your work. Finding where two lines cross takes seconds and catches algebra mistakes."}]},{"say":"Data analysis and geometry questions round out PSAT Math.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Problem solving and data: ratios, rates, percents, unit conversions, mean and median, and reading tables and scatterplots."},{"t":"p","text":"Geometry and trig: area and volume (a reference sheet is provided), angles, triangles, right triangle trigonometry and circles."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A shirt costs $40 and is on sale for 25% off. What's the sale price?","steps":["25% of 40 = 10"],"answer":"$30"},{"t":"p","text":"Watch units carefully and make sure your answer answers the actual question."},{"t":"oops","text":"A discount followed by a markup of the same percent doesn't return to the original price: 100 → 80 → 96 after a 20% discount and then a 20% increase."}]}]];
